<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stamford, Lincolnshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stamford is a market town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-welland">River Welland</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> it was one of the Five Boroughs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, submitting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> in 918. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it prospered from the wool and cloth trade and had many churches and religious houses, and its stone buildings survive largely intact. Burghley House, home of the Cecil family, stands just outside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stamford-lincolnshire.jpg" alt="Stamford, Lincolnshire" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Harrop · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barn_Hill,_Stamford_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7627373.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
